CVA6ConfigDcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0 Signal Flow Tree
=====================================================

1. CONFIGURATION PHASE (cv32a6_imac_sv32_config_pkg.sv)
   └── cva6_cfg.'{ DcacheInvalidateOnFlush: bit'(0) }
       └── Implicitly set to 0 (not explicitly defined in config)

2. BUILD/DERIVATION PHASE (build_config_pkg.sv)
   └── CVA6Cfg.DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0
       └── cfg.DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = CVA6Cfg.DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0

3. MODULE INSTANTIATION PHASE
   └── std_cache_subsystem (core/cache_subsystem/std_cache_subsystem.sv)
       └── wt_cln_dcache_ctrler (core/cache_subsystem/wt_cln_dcache_ctrl.sv)
           └── parameter DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = CONFIG.DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0

4. FLUSH OPERATION BEHAVIOR
   └── Cache Flush Implementation:
       ├── If DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 1:
       │   ├── Flush operation invalidates cache lines
       │   ├── Cache lines marked as invalid after flush
       │   └── Subsequent accesses result in cache misses
       └── If DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0: (CURRENT CONFIGURATION)
           ├── Flush operation does NOT invalidate cache lines
           ├── Cache lines remain valid after flush
           └── Cache hits continue after flush operation

5. FUNCTIONAL IMPACT (DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0)
   └── Flush Behavior:
       ├── Cache flush ensures write-through completion
       ├── All pending writes committed to memory
       ├── Cache lines retain valid data
       ├── No cache warming penalty after flush
       └── Performance maintained post-flush

6. WRITE-THROUGH CACHE CONSIDERATIONS
   └── WT_CLN Cache Behavior:
       ├── All writes immediately go to memory
       ├── Cache lines always contain clean data
       ├── No dirty data to writeback during flush
       ├── Flush primarily ensures write completion
       └── Invalidation not required for coherency

7. PERFORMANCE IMPLICATIONS
   └── Post-Flush Performance (DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0):
       ├── No cache miss penalty after flush
       ├── Maintained cache hit rates
       ├── No cache warming required
       ├── Better performance for flush-heavy scenarios
       └── Reduced memory traffic post-flush

8. COHERENCY CONSIDERATIONS
   └── Multi-agent Coherency:
       ├── Write-through ensures memory is up-to-date
       ├── Other agents see consistent memory state
       ├── Cache invalidation may be required for strict coherency
       └── Configuration suitable for single-core systems

9. CONTROL LOGIC IMPACT
   └── Cache Controller Implementation:
       ├── Simplified flush logic (no invalidation)
       ├── Flush state machine reduced complexity
       ├── No need to clear valid bits during flush
       └── Faster flush completion

10. USE CASE SCENARIOS
    └── When DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0 is appropriate:
        ├── Single-core systems
        ├── Write-through cache with strong ordering
        ├── Performance-critical applications
        └── Systems without cache coherency requirements

    └── When DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 1 might be needed:
        ├── Multi-core systems with cache coherency
        ├── Systems with shared memory regions
        ├── Strict memory consistency requirements
        └── Debug/test scenarios requiring cache invalidation

KEY RELATIONSHIPS:
- DcacheInvalidateOnFlush = 0 preserves cache validity after flush
- Write-through cache reduces need for invalidation
- Improves post-flush performance by avoiding cache misses
- Suitable for single-core systems with write-through caches
- Simplifies cache controller flush logic